<td id="S5.T4.2.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.6.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E8E8E8;">1.6464</span><span id="S5.T4.2.1.6.6.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E8E8E8;"> <span id="S5.T4.2.1.6.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#41719C;">(<math id="S5.T4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#41719C;--ltx-bg-color:#E8E8E8;" mathbackground="#E8E8E8" mathcolor="#41719C">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>1.08)</span></span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Settings.</span> We measure efficiency on HFuzzer. For each HFuzzer round, we evaluate the five models introduced in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Models ‣ 4. Experimental Setup ‣ PackMonitor: Enabling Zero Package Hallucinations Through Decoding-Time Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> with <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PackMonitor</span> and all the training-free baselines introduced in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Baselines ‣ 4. Experimental Setup ‣ PackMonitor: Enabling Zero Package Hallucinations Through Decoding-Time Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, and report the average generation time (in seconds).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> The results are summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.2. RQ2: What is the Efficiency Overhead Introduced by PackMonitor? ‣ 5. Experimental Results ‣ PackMonitor: Enabling Zero Package Hallucinations Through Decoding-Time Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and visualized in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2. RQ2: What is the Efficiency Overhead Introduced by PackMonitor? ‣ 5. Experimental Results ‣ PackMonitor: Enabling Zero Package Hallucinations Through Decoding-Time Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20717v1/mean_variance_comparison.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:295/173;" width="295" height="173"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Average generation time and standard deviation of different methods on HFuzzer, aggregated across LLMs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The average overhead is small.</span> Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.2. RQ2: What is the Efficiency Overhead Introduced by PackMonitor? ‣ 5. Experimental Results ‣ PackMonitor: Enabling Zero Package Hallucinations Through Decoding-Time Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that integrating <span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PackMonitor</span> introduces only a modest and consistent slowdown across all five models, with a normalized inference-time ratio between <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.07 and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>1.28</span>. In absolute terms, the added latency is limited: +0.0577s on Qwen2.5-Coder (0.8019s <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 0.8596s, <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>1.07), +0.1187s on Llama-3.1 (1.5277s <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 1.6464s, <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>1.08), +0.2495s on Llama-3 (0.9020s <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 1.1515s, <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>1.28), +0.2542s on DeepSeek-Coder (1.9809s <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 2.2351s, <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>1.13), and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>1.24 on CodeLlama (2.4296s <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 3.0130s). Moreover, <span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PackMonitor</span> is among the most time-efficient methods overall: it achieves the best latency on DeepSeek-Coder, Qwen2.5-Coder, and CodeLlama, and the second-best latency on Llama-3.1 and Llama-3. In contrast, RAG and SR often incur substantially higher overhead, and RAG+SR can be particularly expensive (e.g., up to <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>2.65 on DeepSeek-Coder).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The latency variance is small and stable.</span> Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2. RQ2: What is the Efficiency Overhead Introduced by PackMonitor? ‣ 5. Experimental Results ‣ PackMonitor: Enabling Zero Package Hallucinations Through Decoding-Time Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> further reports the average generation time and standard deviation aggregated across models. Compared with SR and RAG+SR, <span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PackMonitor</span> exhibits noticeably smaller variance, indicating that its overhead is more predictable and does not suffer from occasional latency spikes. This stability is expected because <span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PackMonitor</span> performs only lightweight per-step operations (DFA state transitions and logits masking) during decoding, whereas SR and RAG+SR may introduce variable amounts of additional computation due to refinement iterations (and RAG also adds retrieval-related variability).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS2.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="116.99" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 116.99" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,116.99) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 2.08 5.88 L 2.08 106.96 C 2.08 111.36 5.64 114.92 10.03 114.92 L 471.5 114.92 C 475.89 114.92 479.45 111.36 479.45 106.96 L 479.45 5.88 C 479.45 1.49 475.89 -2.08 471.5 -2.08 L 10.03 -2.08 C 5.64 -2.08 2.08 1.49 2.08 5.88 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0F0;" fill="#F0F0F0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 6.92 L 0 110.08 C 0 113.9 3.1 116.99 6.92 116.99 L 470.46 116.99 C 474.28 116.99 477.38 113.9 477.38 110.08 L 477.38 6.92 C 477.38 3.1 474.28 0 470.46 0 L 6.92 0 C 3.1 0 0 3.1 0 6.92 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.01 12.24)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.33em;--ltx-fo-height:6.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="92.52" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 92.52)" width="447.36"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS2.p6.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.33em;">
<span id="S5.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Answer to RQ2.<span id="S5.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
On HFuzzer, <span id="S5.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PackMonitor</span> incurs only a small and stable efficiency overhead, whereas existing training-free baselines introduce noticeably higher and more variable time costs.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>RQ3: Does <span id="S5.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PackMonitor</span> Degrade the General Code Generation Capability of LLMs?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is crucial to verify that our proposed <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PackMonitor</span> does not compromise the model’s general code generation capabilities. To this end, we evaluate whether <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PackMonitor</span> affects functional correctness on a standard code generation benchmark.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5. </span>Comparison of code generation quality (Pass@1) on HumanEval. Values in parentheses represent the performance drop relative to the vanilla model.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
